      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Evidence of the moderating role that compulsive exercise may provide in contributing to ED pathology in mid-distance runners confirms a need to better understand pathological motivation in exercise behavior."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "This study had several limitations worth noting. As mentioned previously, it is possible that individuals registered for the half marathon in the current study sample were of a more elite running status. While the current study identified distinct differences between categorical groupings, race-length should only be a preliminary proxy for investigating differences between athletes who are elite vs. non-elite; future work might include self-report on running history or perceived elite status within analyses. This study was also dependent on cross-sectional, self-reported data collection methods; future work should investigate the relation between the study variables over time, allowing for investigation of the impact of athlete injury. For example, relative inactivity during recuperation from injury may positively impact compulsive exercise upon return, as well as increase ED pathology, related to weight and shape concern when sedentary. As well, this study used a self-report measure reporting over the past 28-days (i.e., EDE-Q). Accordingly, it should be noted that scores on this measure might have been affected, and perhaps elevated, given increased training and desire for fitness given proximity to race day. Finally, the runners assessed were all adults who were registered for competitive events in the northeastern United States, and results may not necessarily generalize to other age groups, or geographical areas, including internationally. Given developmental factors and typical age of onset for ED, it may be important to investigate variables within the current study amongst runners who are adolescent and young-adult."
    },
